1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,060
In 1590, governor John White returned to

2
00:00:04,060 --> 00:00:06,840
an English settlement on Roanoke Island.

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:09,320
He expected to find colonists,

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:10,960
including his family.

5
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:13,840
Instead, the site was deserted.

6
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:17,620
A word associated with Croatoan was carved

7
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:18,420
at the site.

8
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:20,740
It offered a possible clue,

9
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:23,900
but not a complete explanation of where

10
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:25,145
everyone had gone.

11
00:00:25,795 --> 00:00:28,420
White had been delayed for years by

12
00:00:28,420 --> 00:00:31,740
war and supply difficulties across the Atlantic.

13
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:34,640
The colony had spent that time without

14
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:35,900
the help it expected.

15
00:00:37,020 --> 00:00:38,640
The settlers may have moved,

16
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:40,680
joined Indigenous communities,

17
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:43,180
or faced several hardships.

18
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:47,460
Historians cannot reconstruct the full fate of

19
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:49,240
every person from the evidence.

20
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:53,300
The popular phrase lost colony centers the

21
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:57,020
English record. Indigenous peoples already lived in

22
00:00:57,020 --> 00:00:59,700
the region, and their history is not

23
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:02,708
an empty backdrop to a European mystery.

24
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:06,000
Roanoke remains unresolved,

25
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:09,240
but absence of a surviving English account

26
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:12,180
is not proof of supernatural disappearance.

27
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:15,400
The true story is about isolation,

28
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:18,320
contact, and uncertain evidence.
